 * `source: "github"`, and `scripts/verify-gamemods.ts`'s own header comment
 * says GitHub was deliberately left unwired ("no lightweight give-me-this-
 * project's-metadata endpoint the way Modrinth/Spiget do... wiring those up is
 * real, separate follow-up work") — which is why all 22 rows have sat with
 * `license: null` and `verifiedAt: null` since they were seeded. That's not
 * just a cosmetic gap: CS2's ecosystem is unlicensed-by-default far more often
 * than Modrinth's (plenty of real, popular, actively-maintained CS2 plugin
 * repos ship with no LICENSE file at all), and this catalogue makes no
 * copyright claim either way without checking. This script is that follow-up:
 * it verifies/refreshes every existing GitHub-sourced row for a family AND
 * discovers new ones, both through the same real GitHub REST API.
 *
 * SOURCE: GitHub REST API v3, https://docs.github.com/en/rest — plus, for CS2
 * specifically, a real curated seed list (github.com/samyycX/awesome-cs2's
 * README, fetched raw off raw.githubusercontent.com — not the GitHub API, so
 * it costs nothing against the API rate limit) which is more reliable for
 * this game than topic search alone (verified live 2026-09-20: search only
 * turns up 57 `cs2-plugin`-tagged repos; the curated list has 121 across its
 * three real plugin-framework sections and catches things authors never
 * tagged).
 *
 * TWO KINDS OF WORK PER GAME, run together in one pass:
 *   1. VERIFY existing rows — every GameMod row already in this family with
 *      source "github" gets looked up by its real `sourceId` ("owner/repo")
 *      and, if the repo still resolves, has its verified-fact fields
 *      (license, description, iconUrl/authorAvatarUrl, pushed-date-derived
 *      upstreamOk, ...) refreshed. Hand-curated fields — name, category,
 *      popularity, featured — are NEVER touched here; only
 *      scripts/verify-gamemods.ts's "verified upstream facts" section is.
 *      A repo that 404s gets `upstreamOk: false` (never deleted — same
 *      convention as verify-gamemods.ts).
 *   2. DISCOVER new rows — real repos found via a curated list and/or GitHub
 *      topic search that pass the quality gate and aren't already in the DB
 *      (by sourceId) get created fresh, with name/category/popularity/loaders
 *      derived from real signals (repo name, curated section, topics,
 *      stargazers_count — never fabricated).
 *
 * MATCHING KEY: `sourceId` ("owner/repo", GitHub's own `full_name`), looked up
 * case-insensitively — NOT the `slug` the `@@unique([gameFamily, slug])`
 * constraint is actually built on. GitHub repos get renamed/transferred more
 * than Modrinth slugs do, and matching on a stable API-native identifier
 * before falling back to slug generation avoids ever creating a second row
 * for the same repo just because this script's slugify differs from
 * whichever human picked the original curated slug (e.g. seed-gamemods.ts's
 * "vipcore" for `partiusfabaa/cs2-vip` — a curated display name, not a
 * mechanical slugify of the repo name).
 *
 * QUALITY GATE (spam filter). Per this session's own research: star count
 * ALONE is not reliable (a suspicious high-star/no-description repo turned up
 * in a live topic search this session). A repo is imported only if ALL of:
 *   - it has a real description (>= 15 chars, not just whitespace/emoji)
 *   - it is not archived
 *   - it was pushed to within the last MAX_STALE_DAYS (730 days / ~2 years)
 * Star count is stored as a real signal (capped `popularity`), never used as
 * the sole admission test.
 *
 * LICENSE GATE — Nxeon legal policy set this session, applied literally:
 * GitHub content is MIRROR-SAFE only when the repo's own license object
 * carries a confirmed permissive SPDX id (see PERMISSIVE_SPDX below).
 * `spdx_id: "NOASSERTION"` (GitHub's "other"/could-not-classify bucket) and no
 * license at all are both treated as NOT confirmed-permissive. Per this
 * session's explicit instruction, non-permissive repos are not skipped: they
 * are imported "link-out-only" (same fetch-not-mirror pattern this codebase
 * already uses for non-permissive Thunderstore/Modrinth content — we only
 * ever store metadata + a live-resolved link to the AUTHOR'S OWN GitHub
 * release asset, worker/game-mods.ts's resolveUrl(), never a rehosted copy),
 * with the real license string stored as-is and `upstreamNote` saying plainly
 * that it isn't confirmed permissive. The `license` field NEVER says
 * something is permissive when it isn't — see `classifyLicense` below.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOES NOT FETCH (by design, see resolveUrl() in
 * worker/game-mods.ts): the latest release / its download asset. Resolving a
 * download is the WORKER's job, done fresh at install time (GET
 * /repos/{owner}/{repo}/releases/latest) — exactly like modrinth's resolveUrl
 * already does — so nothing here goes stale the moment an author cuts a new
 * release, and this script's own GitHub API budget (see next section) isn't
 * spent on a call whose result would just be thrown away by the time anyone
 * actually installs the mod.
 *
 * RATE LIMIT — THE BINDING CONSTRAINT THIS PASS. Confirmed live 2026-09-20:
 * no GITHUB_TOKEN in /opt/nxeon/.env, so this runs unauthenticated: 60 REST
 * requests/hour (core), 10 search requests/minute — a real, tight budget
 * against ~150+ real CS2 plugin repos across the curated list + topic search.
 * Two things stretch it a long way:
 *   - GitHub's `/search/repositories` response embeds the FULL repo object
 *     (license, description, pushed_at, stargazers_count, owner.avatar_url,
 *     topics, archived) per hit — confirmed live this session. Any repo that
 *     turns up in a topic search costs ZERO extra core requests; only repos
 *     found solely via the curated list (not also topic-tagged) need a real
 *     `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}` call.
 *   - `GhClient` tracks the real `X-RateLimit-Remaining` header and stops
